# I18n Conventions

## Use the Framework

Rails ships **I18n** — use **`t()`** / **`I18n.t`** for user-visible static copy instead of scattering raw English in ERB and helpers. Domain data (`@article.title`) stays out of locale files; **labels, buttons, flashes, and validation messages** belong in **`config/locales`**.

Canonical patterns: **`agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-i18n/references/patterns.md`**. Mailer subjects and mail-scoped keys: **`agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-mailers/references/patterns.md`** (subjects) + writing-i18n for key shape. Pundit flash messages: **`agent_harness_rails/skills/writing-policies/references/patterns.md`** § I18n for Policy Messages.

## Keys

- **Mirror the application structure** — locale files and key nesting follow the app's own layout (`articles.index.title` matches `app/views/articles/index.html.erb`) so lazy lookup and Rails' conventional resolution always find the key. Never invent a parallel taxonomy.
- **Scope by feature** — `articles.index.title`, `comments.form.submit`, not a flat `title_472`.
- **`activerecord`** — attributes and errors live under Rails’ expected structure for `human_attribute_name` and form errors.
- **Lazy lookup in templates** — `t(".heading")` resolves relative to the view path; use for page-specific copy.
- **Absolute keys** — shared partials, helpers, controllers (`flash`), mailers — `t("articles.published_notice")`.

## Errors

**All user-facing error messages live in I18n** — never hardcode them in models, controllers, or views. Messages must read as **human language**: override `activerecord.attributes` so schema-shaped names never leak into copy — a join-table attribute like `article_categorizations.category_id` is labelled by its real-world name (`Category`), so errors read “Category must exist”, not “Article categorizations category id can't be blank”.

## Dates and Times

**All user-visible dates and times are formatted through I18n** — `l(date)` / `l(time)` with formats declared under `date.formats` / `time.formats`. No `strftime` in views or helpers.

## Anti-Patterns

| Anti-Pattern | Instead |
|-------------|---------|
| `"Hello " + user.name + "!"` in ERB | `t("greeting", name: user.name)` with `%{name}` in YAML |
| `t("foo.#{key}.bar")` with unbounded `key` | Fixed keys, enum, or explicit allowlist |
| User-facing strings only in English in `en.yml` and hardcoded elsewhere | One approach per app; translate keys consistently |
| `time.strftime("%d/%m/%Y")` in a view | `l(time, format: :short)` with the format in locale YAML |
| Error copy exposing schema names (“Category id can't be blank”) | `activerecord.attributes` override with the real-world name |
| Hardcoded error strings in models/controllers | Keys under `activerecord.errors` / feature namespace |
| Storing canonical copy in the DB for static UI | Locale files for static strings; DB for user-authored content |

## What This Rule Is Not

Translation vendor workflows, TMS sync, and client-side i18n stacks — out of scope. This harness documents **Rails I18n** conventions only.
